Australia is building fewer detached homes than it needs, and wholesale builders are feeling it first. These are the four market signals we are watching, and why they matter if you have lots to sell.

## A structural supply gap

The National Housing Supply and Affordability Council reports gross new supply of about 177,000 dwellings in 2023–24, against new underlying demand of 233,000. The early-2026 outlook expected about 980,000 gross completions during the Accord period, roughly 220,000 short of the 1.2 million target.

That shortfall shows up directly as builder demand for development-ready land.

## House approvals at a near five-year high

Private-sector house approvals rose to 10,537 in May 2026, the highest since September 2021 and the fourth month in a row above 10,000. This is the standardised detached product that wholesale builders construct and package, and they need land to keep up with it.

## Investors are moving into new builds

Investor dwelling lending rose 25.3% year on year to $41.5bn in the March 2026 quarter, 40.3% of the value of new dwelling loan commitments. From 1 July 2027, legislated tax reforms limit negative gearing on future residential investments mainly to qualifying new builds, so investor capital is being redirected into new construction.

## The rental market keeps buyers motivated

National residential vacancy was 1.3% in June 2026, well under the 3% balanced-market threshold, and national asking rents were up 8.1% year on year. That keeps investor interest in off-the-plan new builds strong.

## Where demand is strongest

The fastest-growing submarkets sit in greenfield and outer growth corridors, where wholesale house and land economics work best: Perth's outer ring (Serpentine–Jarrahdale, +33.6%), South East Queensland (Logan–Beaudesert, +22–25%) and Adelaide's north (+16.7%). These are the corridors where builders concentrate their activity and where land is scarcest.

## What it means for land developers

Kenekt aggregates stock from more than 70 wholesale builders who have ready-to-build capacity and are looking for land partners. Positioning your lots through that channel puts them in front of builders with buyer pipelines, 600+ qualified buyer's agents and the referral networks behind them, often before titles are issued.
